+# Underwater learning — revised visionOS specification
+
+## Task and presentation
+
+Support reading a marine lesson and inspecting its selected specimen while seated in a small room. Reading, specimen selection, and settings begin in a movable, dynamically scaled Shared Space window with passthrough intact. Reading never opens an immersive space. A contained 3D specimen preview may accompany the lesson; depth helps inspection but does not justify replacing the room. The complete lesson remains usable without entering the reef.
+
+Optional reef exploration uses progressive Full Space: environmental context helps relate the specimen to its habitat. Do not offer automatic full immersion or silently escalate coverage. No task requires walking, standing, turning around, or reaching overhead.
+
+## Entry and intensity contract
+
+1. The lesson window shows the selected specimen, lesson position, and a secondary, explicit **Enter Reef** button. Adjacent copy: “Explore this habitat while seated. Adjust immersion with the Digital Crown. Return to Lesson keeps your place.” Merely reading, selecting, or reopening a lesson never activates this button.
+2. On activation, capture lesson ID, reading position, specimen ID, inspection orientation, and completed learning steps. Begin progressive immersion at its lowest supported coverage, retaining visible surroundings. Stage the stationary reef and controls before revealing the environment through a predictable fade; never simulate diving, rolling, or moving the viewer.
+3. While opening, show “Opening Reef…” and suppress duplicate opens. **Return to Lesson** remains actionable and cancels pending entry. Failure leaves the lesson and selection intact, with “Reef couldn’t open” and an explicit **Try Again** action; never retry into immersion automatically.
+4. Inside the reef, visible guidance says “Turn the Digital Crown to adjust immersion.” The Crown controls progressive coverage; preserve system recentering, temporary visibility, obstacle awareness, and boundary behavior. Do not impose a competing app gesture or restore a higher saved coverage on reentry. Ambient animation is separate from immersion coverage.
+5. Keep **Return to Lesson**, **Pause Animation**, and **Open Settings** on a central, space-anchored control surface. Settings opens ordinary contained controls beside the lesson content in the current context, with **Done** returning to exploration; it never requires travel to a reef location. Settings includes explanatory Crown guidance. No app intensity slider or custom Crown replacement is specified.
+
+## Stable scene and stationary interaction
+
+Remove all automatic camera roll, world rotation, bobbing, simulated swimming, and forced viewpoint translation. The viewpoint follows the person's physical pose through the platform. Use a stable horizon and stationary seabed ground-plane mesh with coherent scale and lighting; floor-based content aligns to a horizontal floor plane. Keep background contrast and peripheral detail restrained. No large creatures or effects sweep across the visual field.
+
+Bring each selected specimen to the same central inspection area using a short fade, without translating the world. **Next Specimen**, **Previous Specimen**, **Rotate Specimen Left**, **Rotate Specimen Right**, and **Reset Specimen** provide discrete alternatives to direct manipulation; rotate only the specimen on explicit activation. Preserve its ID and orientation across presentation changes. Brief direct manipulation may supplement these actions. Essential controls work with indirect activation and hands resting in the lap or at the sides.
+
+**Pause Animation** immediately stops nonessential animal, plant, and particle movement and changes its label to **Resume Animation**. This affects ambience, never lesson progress or system pose tracking. Honor Reduce Motion on entry and when it changes: suppress decorative motion and animated depth changes, use restrained fades for scene/specimen changes, and preserve all learning information in text and static specimen views. Under Reduce Motion, Resume cannot override that preference; explain “Animation reduced by system setting.” Do not queue animation transitions: the latest action supersedes earlier transitions and exit interrupts immediately. These choices reduce risk; comfort still requires worn-device testing.
+
+## Exit, interruption, and restoration
+
+**Return to Lesson** means leave Full Space and restore the Shared Space lesson, not quit the app. It is always available during exploration, settings, and entry transitions; no confirmation or mandatory ending animation blocks it. Stop ambience and optional animation, dismiss the immersive space, and restore the same selected specimen, inspection orientation, reading position, and completed steps. A persistent “Lesson restored” status confirms completion without relying on audio.
+
+Update a durable lesson checkpoint on selection, reading-position changes, and learning-step completion, independently of scene teardown. On system dismissal or app interruption, pause exploration and retain that checkpoint. On relaunch, open the Shared Space lesson at that checkpoint; reentering the reef always requires Enter Reef again. A system action that temporarily reveals surroundings is not an instruction to erase the lesson or immediately reopen immersion. If a selected specimen is no longer available, retain the lesson position and explain the missing specimen with a choice of replacement rather than silently choosing one.
+
+Progressive/full immersion has an approximately 1.5 m system boundary from initial head position; this is awareness behavior, not a required play area or a guarantee of obstacle clearance. Respect system fading/passthrough and never encourage walking to regain content. If a future activity requires movement beyond that range, redesign it for mixed immersion before shipping. This stationary design does not require room scanning or custom hand tracking; retain real hands and do not request sensitive sensing merely for decoration.
+
+## Controls and access
+
+Use standard visionOS buttons with visible shape/fill, standard feedback, and accessible action names matching their labels. Enter Reef is secondary to reading; Return to Lesson is prominent in the reef and nondestructive. All other commands are ordinary actions. Do not bind generic Return-key activation to immersive entry. Pause/Resume exposes the current animation state as well as its action name.
+
+Keep the lesson, inspection object, and active controls in comfortable central view at coherent depths. Anchor them to space, never rigidly to the head. Allow window placement and preserve system recentering. Prefer one control surface over scattered labels. Use dynamically scaled interactive surfaces; do not prescribe a universal viewing angle or fixed viewing distance. Use 60 × 60 pt activation regions for buttons, at least 60 pt center spacing and 16 pt visible separation; enlarge spacing where necessary to avoid target or hover overlap. Visual button size and activation size are distinct.
+
+Provide a named specimen selection and text description so spatial visuals alone do not carry learning content. All essential actions must be reachable through supported assistive navigation and activation, including hand/head Pointer Control and Switch Control, without speech or precise direct manipulation. Support Zoom and text enlargement to at least 200% with reflow and no loss of exit/settings actions. Default lesson text uses platform text styles (visionOS default 17 pt); do not shrink text to fit. Preserve contrast over the reef with a stable readable control background and check Increase Contrast. Audio ambience yields to other audio; any essential audible information also has text. No haptic feedback is assumed on visionOS.
+
+## Design basis
+
+These are product requirements chosen using the locally installed HIG snapshot, not verified SDK behavior. Relevant Apple articles: [Designing for visionOS — Best practices](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/designing-for-visionos#Best-practices), [Immersive experiences — immersion, comfort, transitions, environment](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/immersive-experiences), [Motion — visionOS](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/motion#visionOS), [Spatial layout](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/spatial-layout), [Accessibility](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/accessibility), and [Buttons — visionOS](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/buttons#visionOS). Implementation must verify target SDK support and actual transition behavior before claiming conformance.
